00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:13.150
machine.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.550
So the reason I
use this a lot is

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.630
because I'm just starting
out my application,

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:23.170
as a developer, an IT Ops.

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:25.090
I'm heavily relying
on Cloud Shell

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.720
to manage my Cloud
resources and configurations

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.800
in my first few stages
of my application.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.215
So when I say that I want to
deploy my application in GCP,

00:04:35.215 --> 00:04:36.590
what I really mean
is that I want

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.850
to put my code and my services
in Google's infrastructure.

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:43.550
We have over 19 regions
around the globe,

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:44.960
and where we are
right now, we're

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.430
actually closest to
a Los Angeles region.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.760
So I'm just starting
out my application,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.370
and I want to deploy
to my region closest

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.220
to my customer, Los Angeles.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.380
But of course, as my
application grows,

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.640
I will have customers
all over the world.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.610
So I will be deploying
to multiple regions

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:04.460
in the near future.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.880
This is a very
simple architecture

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.260
that we currently have now.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:14.300
Think of it as I deployed a VM
instance in US-west2, the Los

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.690
Angeles region, and
within that region,

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:20.850
I've deployed my VM in
zone A. So with the least

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.420
amount of effort, I replicated
my local environment

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.470
to an environment in the Cloud.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:32.960
It is currently running a web
server and a MySQL database.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.820
The user can access this
solution through a domain name,

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.080
for example, mywebapp.com,
and the traffic

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:43.900
will be resolved at the
external IP attached to IBM.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.050
Now the user can start
testing my application,

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:48.520
testing my alpha phase.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.430
This is the simplest solution
on Google Cloud right now.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:55.820
Think of it as the lift
and shift migration.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:57.950
Now that we moved
into stage two,

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:03.787
and I have around 10K users, I'm
experiencing some odd behavior

00:06:03.787 --> 00:06:04.745
and performance issues.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:09.770
I can summarize that into
three types of issues.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:13.940
The single one is
single instance failure.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.910
Since I only have a
single VM and there

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:19.352
might be different
types of errors

00:06:19.352 --> 00:06:20.810
that occur on that
single instance,

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.450
think of it as VM issues,
web service issues,

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:24.920
or database issues.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:27.440
Whenever that happens, I need
to either restart my services

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:29.120
or reboot my VM.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.910
What that really means
is that there is downtime

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.300
to my whole application.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.160
This is the cause of a
single instance failure.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.140
So another type of issue
is a single zone failure.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:45.820
Imagine that my VM is hosted
in zone A of the Los Angeles

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:46.810
region.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:50.410
For some unlikely reason
something happens to that zone,

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:53.740
and I don't have any backup
instances in another zone.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:57.830
This will, again, cause my
whole application downtime.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:00.030
And then finally,
scalability issues.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:04.730
So as my application
grows, I have more users

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:09.685
concurrently accessing my
application, which might cause

00:07:09.685 --> 00:07:12.280
some inconsistent behaviors.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.110
That really can
be avoided since I

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:18.400
don't have any monitoring
or auto healing or anything

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.220
within my single VM right now.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:24.250
So based on these
issues, we can observe

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:26.230
that my current architecture
is really flawed

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:29.260
with single point failure--

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.930
meaning one failure will
cause my whole application,

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:33.910
my whole system, to go down.

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:39.020
Based on all of that,
what I really need

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:43.640
is to build a scalable and
resilient web application.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:46.400
Scalable simply means
that my application

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.250
needs to work with one
user all the way up to 100

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:51.380
million users.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:53.420
Resilient means
that my application

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:55.260
needs to be highly available.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:57.050
It needs to continue
to function when

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:59.900
an unexpected failure happens.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:04.250
For example, if
the VM has issues

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:07.670
or if the zone
experiences failure,

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.640
my application needs to
remain fault tolerant.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:16.256
Harry is going to give us some
ideas of how to do that in GCP.

00:08:16.256 --> 00:08:17.030
HARRY LIN: OK.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:17.900
Thanks, Helen.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:21.470
It's good to see your business
grow to over 10,000 users.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:23.930
This is the simple
architecture that Helen

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:25.910
is running on Google Cloud.

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:27.530
We start small,
and the provision,

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:30.010
only one single VM instance.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:33.890
As the application grows,
the number of VM instances

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:34.966
grows as well.

00:08:34.966 --> 00:08:36.799
So we can find that it
is getting difficult,

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:39.309
but still manageable.

00:08:39.309 --> 00:08:42.970
Now, what if you need to scale
over tens of VM instances?

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:47.430
And when one of them goes
down, you have to take action.

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:49.380
Or when traffic
dips, you suddenly

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:53.770
have all of these
servers, which are idle.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:57.400
So Managed Instance Group
comes into the solution

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:02.510
to assist with auto-scaling
and auto-healing instances.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:06.940
Manage Instance Group contains
a single base instance template.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:10.340
When the service needs to
scale out for new instance,

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.670
Manage Instance Group
can create a new instance

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:14.680
from this base template.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:18.903
In addition, Manage Instance
Group has three major features.

00:09:18.903 --> 00:09:20.070
The first one is autoscaler.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:24.670
Autoscaler can create
and destroy VM instances

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:28.060
based on the loading, which
is defined by yourself.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:31.420
For example, you can use the
network throughput, CPU usage,

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:34.480
and memory usage as
the scale in metric.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.610
The second one is auto-healer.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.250
Auto-healer will
recreate the instance

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.320
which becomes unhealthy.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:45.920
Unhealthy can be also defined by
yourself with the custom health

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.730
check.

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:49.850
The third one is
regional distribution.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:52.140
The regional
distribution-- you can

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:54.150
distribute all of
the VM instances

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:58.650
across the multiple zones which
makes your solution more highly

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.090
available.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.100
Looking at these three
separate features,

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:07.940
these actually solve the
previous issues that we have--

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.710
scalability and
high availability.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:15.270
With autoscaler we can make sure
that your application is always

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:17.160
scaling to the current zone.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:21.990
With auto-healer and the
regional distribution

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.860
your application can
recreate unhealthy instances

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.510
and avoid single zone failure.

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:34.210
Now that we have Managed
Instance Group config,

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:37.765
we need to distribute your
traffic across all the VM

00:10:37.765 --> 00:10:39.670
instances within the group.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:43.540
This is where the were load
balancer comes into play.

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:45.700
Using the original
Managed instance Group,

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:49.510
we can deploy your instances
across multiple zones.

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:52.870
Load balancer will spread
the traffic across all the VM

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:56.350
instances within
the group, and there

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:59.890
is the single Anycast IP
attached to this network load

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:01.000
balancer.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:06.820
So all of your users will
access this fixed Anycast IP

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:12.225
in order to access a fleet of
instances behind this Match

00:11:12.225 --> 00:11:12.850
Instance Group.

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:18.870
Now our service
is scalable, able

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:22.270
to handle traffic and
unexpected outages,

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:24.150
but I'm sure that
many of you will

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:28.500
wonder how this staple
service is scalable.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.630
Take database as an example.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.190
In previous
architecture, you can

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:35.610
see that we run all of the
services on the same VM

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:36.940
instance.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:39.020
This is half the scale
in point of view,

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:42.330
and also harmful to
service availability.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.610
Imagine that one
instance is down

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:47.595
which costs the entire service
running on the same instance

00:11:47.595 --> 00:11:50.850
to be down as well.

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.450
Hence, this is why
architecture evolves.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:58.620
We decouple the application
into the typical three tier web

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.240
applications.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.630
The frontend, the backend
and the third base layer.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:06.990
Manage Instance Group is
configured to both frontend

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.530
and backend service to
provide scalability and high

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.030
availability.

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:17.280
At each layer, we deployed a VM
instance across multiple zones

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:20.180
to cope with the
single zone failure.

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:22.760
At the frontend we have
the Cloud load balancer

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:25.910
to distribute the
traffic to the frontend,

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:29.510
and we use the internal load
balancer to spread the traffic

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:33.320
to the backend service as we
deploy our backend service

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.970
into the private network.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:37.530
And the third base layer--

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:41.410
MySQL is running on Compute
Engines with the HA mode.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:44.610
This provides the master
to slave replication

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:48.330
for HA purposes,
and you re-replica

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:53.850
can be added manually to
improve the performance.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:56.620
And don't forget the monitoring
and the logging which easily

00:12:56.620 --> 00:12:59.800
helps you to troubleshoot
your system when something

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.150
goes wrong.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:04.030
Stackdriver is a
comprehensive service

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:07.120
that helps you to
monitor and make

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:10.510
alert policies in
abnormal situations.

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:13.610
If you have auditing or
troubleshooting needs,

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:17.830
the Stackdriver's logging
and tracing abilities

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:19.930
are exactly for that.

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:22.060
In addition,
Stackdriver debugger

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:24.670
can help you to
debug your service

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:27.680
online without
interrupting your service.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:31.930
So in this stage, we address
scalability and resilience

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:35.050
through the Manage Interest
Group and load balancer.

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:38.620
Application is also
decoupled into three tiers

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:41.970
to avoid interruption
between services.

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.200
Stackdriver is a
comprehensive service

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:48.170
to help you monitor,
debug, trace your system.

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:50.020
So I hope that is
helpful to design

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:51.250
your system at this phase.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:52.620
Helen.

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:53.620
HELEN LO: Thanks, Harry.

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:58.810
So now that we have the
common three tier application,

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:04.250
my users have grown 100K, and we
actually run into a new issue.

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:07.390
So looking back at the
current design that we have,

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:10.420
everything is scalable
and highly resilient.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:14.620
So going to 100K has
created a new issue

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:18.070
in my architecture--
specifically, the data layer.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:21.760
So since MySQL database,
right now, is installed,

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:26.590
and I had to manually manage
it on VM in the Compute Engine.

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:29.590
So this is not a very
scalable solution.

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:32.980
As the number of users grow,
the number of concurrent users

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:34.520
grow at the same time.

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.550
So the number of
concurrent reads and writes

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:39.340
to my VMs will
increase, and this

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:42.290
is going to cause some
issues on my data layer.

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:45.490
There are a couple of
ways to improve this.

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:48.520
One, maybe I can
increase the disk

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.160
on my current VM which
will increase IOPS,

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:54.160
or another way is, I can add
another re-replica that Harry

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:56.590
mentioned, but this
a lot of overhead.

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:59.580
We have to install
VMs, manage the VMs,

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.880
and manage the SQL databases.

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:05.440
So I want to take it
as a Cloud approach.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:11.350
So I can, maybe, leverage Google
Cloud's manage SQL services,

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:14.050
or another way is, if I
can offload my database

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:17.510
loading using Redis cache.

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:20.355
So back to the database issue.

00:15:20.355 --> 00:15:22.730
There are many database services
on Google Cloud Platform

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:26.000
right now, and I'm not really
sure which one I should pick.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.790
Which one will be useful
for my current situation?

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:31.190
Harry, what do you think?

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:31.990
HARRY LIN: OK.

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:35.050
Yeah, so database
and storage is one

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:36.640
of the most critical
missions when

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.440
you build out your service.

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:42.760
On Google Cloud there are many
services, storage and database

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:45.950
services, that you can adopt
based on your user cases

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:47.210
and scenarios.

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:49.540
Take database as an example.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.990
You have a choice of Relational
database, NoSQL database,

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:55.000
and the Warehouse, as well.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.850
If you have a typical
transaction to process,

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:02.000
the Relational database will
be a good choice for you.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:05.540
Here, in our case, we will
focus on in-Memory database

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:08.930
and the Relational database
as we need to at this stage.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:13.910
Look at the current
solution, we are running

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:15.770
MySQL on Compute Engine.

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:18.720
It has few areas
that we can improve.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:22.650
We know that database engines
are a fast evolving technology.

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:27.170
So the new, major version
and the minor, past version

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:31.010
keeps coming to enforce the
database demonstrator operating

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:32.760
the software.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:36.700
As users grow, the disk of
your database grows as well.

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:40.760
There is no easy way for
you to extend your disk

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:46.370
space for your database, and
there is no managed re-replica

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:50.540
for you at the database, there,
in previous architecture.

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:56.010
To separate the read and write
operation for your database.

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:59.900
This separation can
also reduce the overhead

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:02.370
of the master database.

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:04.940
So with the managed
service Cloud SQL,

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:08.150
it helps you to smoothly
upgrade your database

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:12.750
in the Maintenance window
that you specified.

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:15.829
When you need to extend
your database disk,

00:17:15.829 --> 00:17:22.619
Cloud SQL can increase your disk
when your disk is running out.

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:25.380
And this disk
increase mechanism,

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:28.530
it doesn't affect
your database--

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:31.620
or you don't need to stop
you database service.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:35.180
You can just
increase on the fly.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:37.940
And also, Cloud SQL
can automatically

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:43.170
increase your disk when your
disk space is running out.

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:45.510
And you can also
define the threshold

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:49.680
that you want to increase or
trigger this automatic disk

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:52.590
space in Mechanism.

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:56.350
Cloud SQL, also, provides
the failover replica

00:17:56.350 --> 00:18:01.900
and the re-replica to help you
easily scale your database.

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:07.420
So failover replica gives you
both the re-ability and also

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.080
high availability.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:12.820
This saves you money without
provision or standby instance

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:15.760
not being able to read.

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:19.300
And last but not least,
the Cloud SQL periodically

00:18:19.300 --> 00:18:21.830
creates a snapshot
of your database

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:25.250
so that you can restore your
database from this managed

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:27.860
backup.

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:30.950
Let's quickly look at
how failover works.

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:34.610
In this diagram, you can see
that the primary instance can

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:39.440
be accessed via IP address
Y, and we have the failover

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:42.920
replica, which can
also be read via IP

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:48.660
address Z. And per region,
additional one, re-replica,

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:51.170
for the read-only
work load, and it

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:54.810
can be accessed
via IP address X.

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:57.080
So every second the
primary instance

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:59.180
will send the heartbeat signal.

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:02.360
If multiple heartbeat
signals are not detecting,

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:06.110
the failover mechanism
is initiated.

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:09.680
The failover mechanism
will be also triggered

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:13.670
when the primary zone
experiences failure.

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:20.730
When a failover is
initiated, Cloud SQL

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:23.820
waits for the failover
replica to catch up

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:26.440
to the primary instance states.

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:28.890
The amount of the
time this state text

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:33.320
is highly affected by
the replication lag.

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:36.460
So here is the final
state after the failover.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:41.110
The failover replica is promoted
to the primary instance row.

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:45.310
The primary instance
name and IP address

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:48.370
are moved to the old
failover replica.

00:19:48.370 --> 00:19:52.450
So the application reconnects
to the primary instance

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:56.200
without changing its original
database connection stream

00:19:56.200 --> 00:20:00.340
because the primary instance
IP address moved automatically

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:02.790
to the old failover replica.

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:08.380
So another point
that Helen mentioned

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:10.390
at the beginning
of this stage, is

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:14.320
to use Cashe to route the
read and write traffic

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:16.870
from database to the cache.

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:21.070
Memorystore for Redis is used
to handle fast read and write

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:22.330
operations.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:25.870
You can keep your frequent
access data in the cache.

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:27.850
Like the frequent access data.

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:29.640
Like the session involved.

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:33.070
You can keep this session
involved in the cache in order

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:36.610
for your application to
access this frequent access

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:37.900
information quickly.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:44.690
It also provides the low
latency for applications

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:47.270
to access these
frequent access data.

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:50.590
It also significantly
uploads the database loading

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:52.600
from your database.

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.060
Memorystore for
Redis also gives you

00:20:55.060 --> 00:21:00.400
the ability to increase
your memory on the fly

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:03.340
without stopping
your cache services.

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:06.010
Of course you also
provide a standby mode

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:11.020
to provide high availability
when the primary instance is

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:12.410
done or the zone failure.

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:20.040
Here we add two types of
databases into the solution.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:24.030
We adapt the fully managed
Relational database service,

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:27.030
Cloud SQL, to easily
create the failover

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:30.190
replica and re-replica.

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:35.620
Memorystore for Redis is also
added between the backend

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:39.790
and database there, in order
to offload the database loading

00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:42.940
and to provide the low
latency for application

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:46.090
to access those
frequent access data.

00:21:46.090 --> 00:21:49.000
The design, at this stage,
we focus on the service.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:52.390
Its ability for your
application instead

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:55.600
of managing your service.

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:59.080
Right now, we address
the database issue,

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:01.810
the scalability, and
the high availability

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:04.420
with Cloud SQL and Memorystore.

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:05.230
Back to you, Helen.

00:22:11.158 --> 00:22:12.920
HELEN LO: Thank you, Harry.

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:17.360
So with you managing
databases, our application

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:22.830
is now more stable, and
we got to 1 million users.

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:24.290
So my e-commerce
website is getting

00:22:24.290 --> 00:22:27.210
very popular, and
the next stage,

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:29.750
we're dealing with
another type of issue.

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:37.390
So looking at my current
architecture right now,

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:41.080
I'm serving my
users globally now.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:42.670
I have 1 million users.

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:46.210
My users are coming
from Europe and Asia.

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:51.520
So my next step is to create
a global, faster web app.

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:53.020
Looking at the
current architecture,

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:57.140
you can see that everything is
hosted in the US-west2 region,

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:59.710
meaning that it's not
really effectively serving

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:01.570
my customers globally.

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:05.890
So my next step is to
replicate this environment

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:09.980
into another region,
for example, US-east

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:14.470
So I did a little research,
and this is a common approach

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:15.850
that I see.

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:19.360
I had my architecture,
my solution,

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:23.950
on the US-west region, and
I did a replica in US-east,

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:27.830
and I use the DNS policy to
split the traffic evenly.

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:30.600
But for some reason,
what if I wanted to add

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:33.490
new VMs in my US-east region?

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:35.995
What I need to do here is I
need to update my DNS policies.

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:42.030
Another issue that might
occur is with DNS cache

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:43.600
by the client.

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:46.650
So what I do here is,
I can set a lower TTL

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:49.350
value to mitigate this issue.

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:54.620
But what if some of the VMs
are experiencing outages?

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:58.290
How should my DNS
policies adapt to that?

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:02.010
And if I'm adding another region
to my solution, for example,

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:07.010
in Europe, I want to use DNS
geolocation routing, instead.

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:08.840
Meaning that users
from Europe will

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.940
be routed through our region.

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:14.330
Users from US-west will be
routed to the US-west region.

00:24:14.330 --> 00:24:16.580
But as you can see,
whenever I need

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:19.280
to add a new region
to my solution

00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:22.600
or I need to add or
edit the loading,

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:24.320
on my architecture
or my solution,

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:28.140
I have to do a lot of
updates to my DNS policies.

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:30.260
So I'm wondering if
there is a better way

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:33.635
to do this in Google
Cloud Platform?

00:24:33.635 --> 00:24:35.090
HARRY LIN: OK.

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:38.990
So while expanding the business
globally is really exciting,

00:24:38.990 --> 00:24:41.310
but it also comes with
some difficulties--

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:43.370
like you want to
manage your service

00:24:43.370 --> 00:24:46.160
across multiple regions.

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.320
So Google Cloud
Global load balancer

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:51.170
is built on top of the
Google Global Network

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:56.060
to spread the traffic of your
users across multiple regions.

00:24:56.060 --> 00:25:00.110
You can configure a single, in
this case, IP or Google virtual

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:04.520
IP, VIP, on Google load
balancer without creating

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:08.000
multiple low balance
IP for each region.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:11.780
This means that you don't need
to manage your DNS policy.

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:15.740
The DNS resolution always point
to the single, in this case,

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:16.550
IP address.

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:22.740
So when your
e-commerce business is

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:25.740
getting popular in
Singapore, for example,

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:30.150
you continue to expand your
service in Singapore region.

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:32.670
So without changing
your DNS policy,

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:37.500
without requiring additional
VIP addresses in new regions,

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:41.190
just one single VIP
across the globe.

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.520
You can add your new services
in the Singapore region

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:49.140
into this global server
load balancing service.

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:52.440
Along with the single
IP, global reach,

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:56.100
you can also get the cross
region failover and spillover

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:58.950
mechanism on the
global load balancer.

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:01.650
For example, in
real case, if you

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:04.850
have all of your instances
in the Singapore region

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:07.530
who goes down,
users in Singapore

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:10.960
will continue to
be served by being

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:15.340
redirected to the application
backend in other regions.

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:20.270
Similarly, if your application
backends in the US-west

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:22.930
are overloaded,
the user's traffic

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:27.730
will be mostly handled by
the application backend

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:30.310
service in the US-east.

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:32.340
So the global load
balancer helps

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:36.880
you to re-balance
your traffic and also,

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:40.690
your available resource
capacity in multiple regions,

00:26:40.690 --> 00:26:43.880
dynamically.

00:26:43.880 --> 00:26:45.740
Along with the
global load balancer,

00:26:45.740 --> 00:26:50.390
you can also enable the Cloud
CDN to accelerate your content

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:52.940
delivery to your end users.

00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:57.350
User experience can be improved
because your content will

00:26:57.350 --> 00:27:01.610
be cached at the local POP
and served to a local user.

00:27:01.610 --> 00:27:06.500
It significantly shortens the
response time to your user,

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:08.930
and also, your static
content can be stored

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:12.500
in the Google Cloud Storage.

00:27:12.500 --> 00:27:17.490
And this static content can be
served at the CDN local POP.

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:20.460
For those dynamic
or static content

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:23.700
generated by your
server, Google Cloud CDN

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:28.380
can reduce the service loading
to avoid sending every request

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:29.680
back to your backend servers.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:37.590
Now we can serve user requests
with the Global Load Balancer,

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:41.160
but can we make our
Relation database

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:44.040
accessible across
multiple regions?

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:45.750
But not every issue is--

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:48.360
most databases
today require making

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:52.770
trade offs between
scalability and consistency.

00:27:52.770 --> 00:27:55.350
So with Cloud Spanner
you get the best

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:57.690
of the relational
database structure

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:03.690
and also non-relational database
scale with strong consistency

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:07.170
across regions and continents.

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:09.810
Cloud Spanner has
regional deployment

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:12.090
and multi-region deployment.

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:15.570
With multi-region
deployment, it automatically

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:18.300
syncs your data across
multiple regions

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:21.390
so that your service
in each region

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:25.430
can access the local
consistent data.

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:28.900
You can scale out,
expand your capacity

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:32.350
by adding additional
instances on the fly

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:34.195
without stopping
Spanner service.

00:28:38.210 --> 00:28:40.160
So finally, this is
our architecture--

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:43.640
to deploy your service
into multiple regions,

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:45.640
to serve global users.

00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:48.920
Global load balancer
has a single IP

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:53.030
for users without
managing your DNS policy.

00:28:53.030 --> 00:28:56.690
Its cross region spillover
and failover mechanisms

00:28:56.690 --> 00:29:00.580
dynamically distribute your user
requests to multiple regions

00:29:00.580 --> 00:29:04.790
when your capacity in
its region changes.

00:29:04.790 --> 00:29:09.320
Along with Cloud CDN, you route
your users to the closest pop

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:12.020
to accelerate your
content delivery,

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:14.960
and Cloud Spanner gives
you a relational database

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:18.730
with global consistency
and scalability.

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:22.990
With this global service,
expanding your global business

00:29:22.990 --> 00:29:26.818
is not difficult anymore.

00:29:26.818 --> 00:29:28.290
HELEN LO: Thank you, Harry.

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:30.750
Wow, now we are serving
our customers globally,

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:33.820
and we have up to 10
million users now.

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:39.020
So if we go back, everything
started with a small startup--

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:40.650
one developer, one VM--

00:29:40.650 --> 00:29:43.300
and now we have
10 million users.

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:47.700
So definitely, we have a lot
more than just one developer.

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:53.040
Now that we are having different
developers, different teams,

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:57.360
ITs, Admin, Ops, we're
constantly updating our code.

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:59.560
We're constantly
deploying our updates,

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:01.500
which is getting
harder to manage

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:04.065
with the new teams and
many people working

00:30:04.065 --> 00:30:05.940
on the same projects,
many developers working

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:07.500
on same projects.

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:10.140
So my next step that I
want to do for my company

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:13.140
is to shift from foreign
monolithic architecture

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:15.370
to a microservices architecture.

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:18.960
So the idea that I have,
is to split engineers

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:20.400
by microservices.

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:23.370
So imagine that I have
five to ten engineers just

00:30:23.370 --> 00:30:26.040
working on the recommendation
engine and another five

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:28.350
to ten engineers working
on the backend or another

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:29.580
for frontend.

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:32.690
So this will allow engineers
with similar technical skills

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:35.940
and experiences to
work in the same team.

00:30:35.940 --> 00:30:39.510
But the end goal is to
improve my development cycle

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:44.320
and make it easier to
manage each service.

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:50.080
So think of this architecture
as a conventional feature

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:50.650
application.

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:54.260
You have the database--
backend and frontend.

00:30:54.260 --> 00:30:58.495
How do I decompose this to a
microservices architecture?

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:02.950
I can decompose
this with looking

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:06.760
at the original application's
services functions.

00:31:06.760 --> 00:31:10.030
For example, I can have a
microservice for authentication

00:31:10.030 --> 00:31:15.260
and a microservice for frontend
and another for backend.

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:18.410
We can kind of see how
my architecture will

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:21.590
look if it's microservice
architecture,

00:31:21.590 --> 00:31:24.440
but how do we manage this
in Google Cloud Platform?

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:26.660
How do we manage the
amount of services

00:31:26.660 --> 00:31:29.793
and to manage all the
growth of microservices?

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:36.900
HARRY LIN: So welcome to the
Microservices and the container

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:37.960
world, Helen.

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:38.520
OK.

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:42.570
So it's great to see your team
expand to a multiple functional

00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:47.190
team, and also, reconsider,
redeploy, or redesign

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:48.870
your service.

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:52.570
To run and manage a
container with microservices,

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:55.890
there are a few things that
we need to think about.

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:59.210
A container lies in the
application platform,

00:31:59.210 --> 00:32:02.330
which can help you focus on
your application development

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:05.690
and deployment, not the
infrastructure management.

00:32:05.690 --> 00:32:09.140
For example, this
includes some VM instances

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:12.950
auto-scaling cluster management
or your container service

00:32:12.950 --> 00:32:14.160
scaling.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:15.950
It should be, also,
easy for your team

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:17.610
to get started with it.

00:32:17.610 --> 00:32:21.390
And also easy for all to
integrate with your existing

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:23.430
CSCD pipeline.

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:26.230
So Google Kubernetes
Engine, GKE,

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:29.150
fixes almost everything
you need here.

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:34.410
GKE is a managed cybernetic
service on Google Cloud.

00:32:34.410 --> 00:32:36.500
It provides the
fundamental features

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:39.920
to help you manage and
deploy your content online

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:41.120
with the service.

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:43.590
For example, for the
auto-scaling cluster,

00:32:43.590 --> 00:32:46.700
auto-scaling, it manages
your cluster master note

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:51.740
and your work note, and it can
scale based on your work note

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:52.660
loading.

00:32:52.660 --> 00:32:56.480
And also it has the horizontal
pop auto-scaling scaling.

00:32:56.480 --> 00:33:01.040
To scale your service based
on the metric you define.

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:03.860
And also to protect your
Google Network cluster.

00:33:03.860 --> 00:33:06.470
It provides the role
based access control,

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:09.290
the private cluster, network
policy, different security

00:33:09.290 --> 00:33:12.630
features to fortify
your cluster.

00:33:12.630 --> 00:33:14.390
So not only is it a
fundamental feature,

00:33:14.390 --> 00:33:16.430
it also has some
advanced features

00:33:16.430 --> 00:33:19.010
to deploy your services.

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:23.160
Let's take the multi-crosser
Ingress as an example

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:27.020
since your VM based
service is already deployed

00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:28.580
across multiple regions.

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:31.390
So you want to deploy
your container lies

00:33:31.390 --> 00:33:34.850
and microservices across
multiple regions as well.

00:33:34.850 --> 00:33:40.190
For microservice, you will need
to deploy your Ingress cluster

00:33:40.190 --> 00:33:43.790
in each region and want
to manage this cluster

00:33:43.790 --> 00:33:47.420
service as a whole to
serve your global user.

00:33:47.420 --> 00:33:50.960
So the GKE multi-cluster
Ingress feature

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:54.710
is an advanced service
that can achieve this goal.

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:58.010
In this diagram, you can find
the web application service

00:33:58.010 --> 00:34:01.490
is deployed into two
clusters which are running

00:34:01.490 --> 00:34:03.750
in two different regions.

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:06.770
With GKE multi-cluster
Ingress, it

00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:12.110
distributes the user's request
to list to different Kubernetes

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:15.800
cluster in different regions
based on the container's

00:34:15.800 --> 00:34:18.210
availability and capacity.

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:22.159
So it has the similar concept
to the Global Load Balancer.

00:34:22.159 --> 00:34:24.889
You can base it on your
past services or container

00:34:24.889 --> 00:34:29.570
services of availability
and also, the capacity

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:33.440
to dynamically adjust the
request to different regions.

00:34:36.489 --> 00:34:40.570
And in addition to the container
orchestration platform,

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:42.755
the CI/CD also
delivers a pipeline,

00:34:42.755 --> 00:34:46.840
which is also important for your
development team and operation

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:51.590
team to simultaneously
collaborate together.

00:34:51.590 --> 00:34:54.489
On the Google Cloud, the
Cloud Source repository

00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:59.440
offers a managed source
control repository service

00:34:59.440 --> 00:35:03.670
which can trigger the Cloud
to build a new region based

00:35:03.670 --> 00:35:05.420
on the criteria you define.

00:35:05.420 --> 00:35:09.370
For example, if you push the
code with the new revision

00:35:09.370 --> 00:35:12.810
and it has the
specific text then you

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:16.380
want to trigger somebody
to build a new region.

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:17.760
That's one example.

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:22.290
And you can register your
container in the Cloud register

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:24.840
and integrate with
the spinning curve

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:27.220
for continuous deployment.

00:35:27.220 --> 00:35:29.770
When the container
of the new revision

00:35:29.770 --> 00:35:33.100
is available in
the Cloud register,

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:38.170
it can trigger a new
Spinnaker deployment pipeline,

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:41.720
and this deployment pipeline
can deploy your service

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:47.450
into the App Engine, the GKE,
or even the Compute Engine.

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:49.340
And after the
deployment, you can

00:35:49.340 --> 00:35:53.480
use the Statedriver to
monitor your deployment status

00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:56.210
and also, service status.

00:35:56.210 --> 00:36:03.810
This completes the cycle of the
[INAUDIBLE] of delivery on GCP,

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:06.510
and it could also
be possible that you

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:10.650
are development or operation
team already has their existing

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:14.380
solution in the CI/CD pipeline.

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:19.260
So one of the unique
values to GCP and the GKE,

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:22.110
as well, is openness.

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:25.890
So Google open source
many Google services

00:36:25.890 --> 00:36:30.310
like the TensorFlow, like
Kubernetes, Apache Beam.

00:36:30.310 --> 00:36:32.910
Therefore, our managed
service are also

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:37.970
open to smoothly integrate
with this open source project.

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:41.120
In this diagram, you can
see that at each stage

00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:43.220
of the delivery
pipeline, you can

00:36:43.220 --> 00:36:45.800
use the different
open source projects

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:48.800
to integrate with the
Google managed service.

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:51.320
This gives you the
flexibility to create

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:53.186
[INAUDIBLE] CI/CD pipeline.

00:36:56.840 --> 00:37:00.710
So the architecture evolves as
this high level architecture

00:37:00.710 --> 00:37:01.920
diagram shows.

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:07.820
The GKE now is managing all the
containerized microservices,

00:37:07.820 --> 00:37:10.900
and we create a CI/CD
pipeline to manage

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:14.720
application development,
deployment, and operation.

00:37:14.720 --> 00:37:19.820
This development team can
use these CI/CD pipeline

00:37:19.820 --> 00:37:23.870
to push their code and build
the new revision package.

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:26.480
Operation team can
deploy a new revision

00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:29.510
into the staging or the
production environment

00:37:29.510 --> 00:37:32.570
and use the different tools
to monitor your deployment

00:37:32.570 --> 00:37:34.870
and the service status.

00:37:34.870 --> 00:37:36.860
So, Helen, I think
this architecture

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:40.190
can help you to manage your
containerized Microservices

00:37:40.190 --> 00:37:43.445
and the CI/CD pipeline on GCP.

00:37:43.445 --> 00:37:44.650
HELEN LO: Thank you, Harry.

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:46.690
So we're at the last stage.

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:49.330
We have 100 million users now.

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:51.040
So in this section,
we're actually

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:53.980
going to discuss something that
we haven't talked about yet.

00:37:53.980 --> 00:37:56.580
Data, or in our case, big data.

00:37:56.580 --> 00:38:00.580
Imagine that we have 100 million
users on my application now,

00:38:00.580 --> 00:38:03.410
and there is a lot of
data to be captured.

00:38:03.410 --> 00:38:05.560
So for this section,
I want to see

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:09.510
what kind of services in Google
Cloud can help me with that.

00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:11.810
So let's start to think
about what kind of data

00:38:11.810 --> 00:38:14.480
can be captured on the
e-commerce website.

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:16.190
From the frontend
or UI perspective,

00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:19.970
I can have click stream
or user view history--

00:38:19.970 --> 00:38:21.950
how long a user
stays at each page.

00:38:21.950 --> 00:38:24.200
From the backend
side, I can look

00:38:24.200 --> 00:38:29.290
at items added to a shopping
cart or the order history.

00:38:29.290 --> 00:38:31.800
But with all the data
collected, what I really

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:35.340
want is to understand my
users and get the business

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:38.220
insights so that I can
answer questions such as--

00:38:38.220 --> 00:38:40.440
I want to create a better
recommendation engine

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:44.850
or I want to understand my user
and do a user segmentation.

00:38:44.850 --> 00:38:47.320
So looking back on the data.

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:50.250
I can actually categorize
them in two types--

00:38:50.250 --> 00:38:51.990
streaming and batch data.

00:38:51.990 --> 00:38:54.930
Streaming data are the
types like, click stream,

00:38:54.930 --> 00:38:56.680
that's coming in our real time.

00:38:56.680 --> 00:39:02.070
And for batch data, think of
it as logs or database data.

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:05.160
So a lot of these
data are actually

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:08.570
captured on a very large scale.

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:11.120
I'm not really sure how to
do this on Google Cloud.

00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:12.828
What kind of services
can help with that?

00:39:15.595 --> 00:39:16.430
HARRY LIN: OK.

00:39:16.430 --> 00:39:18.370
So wow, 100 million users.

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:19.740
That is amazing.

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:23.720
So with 100 million users, you
can imagine how large a volume

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:26.280
you need to ingest
and the process.

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:29.870
So imagine each click
and the View History

00:39:29.870 --> 00:39:35.170
are the streaming data
generated by your user actions,

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:38.810
and also, no matter if he
or she visits your website,

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:42.050
clicks on a popular
item, then you will be

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:44.160
generating one streaming data.

00:39:44.160 --> 00:39:48.170
So no need to mention
that how these huge data

00:39:48.170 --> 00:39:52.580
volumes will be generated on
like the Black Friday or Cyber

00:39:52.580 --> 00:39:54.210
Monday.

00:39:54.210 --> 00:39:56.750
So for such tremendous
data, you need

00:39:56.750 --> 00:40:00.860
to ingest the Google
Cloud pop up service

00:40:00.860 --> 00:40:03.860
could be your best choice.

00:40:03.860 --> 00:40:07.490
Cloud pop up service can
scale out automatically

00:40:07.490 --> 00:40:10.260
based on you ingested volume.

00:40:10.260 --> 00:40:13.010
And also, a guaranteed
at least once

00:40:13.010 --> 00:40:16.030
delivered for your
subscriber for you

00:40:16.030 --> 00:40:18.620
a further processing afterwards.

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:23.300
And for batch data, you can
already store your assets lock

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:27.430
in the Google Cloud Storage
and some transitional data

00:40:27.430 --> 00:40:31.150
it can store in the relational
database like Cloud SQL

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:33.050
or Spanner.

00:40:33.050 --> 00:40:35.690
So after it ingests
the streaming data,

00:40:35.690 --> 00:40:39.590
the Cloud Dataflow can help
you with processing your data

00:40:39.590 --> 00:40:42.890
before you store its data
into your data warehouse.

00:40:42.890 --> 00:40:46.790
For example, you may want to
remove some duplicate data,

00:40:46.790 --> 00:40:49.940
formalize or normalize
your data set,

00:40:49.940 --> 00:40:53.630
and afterwards, you will
store all this normalized data

00:40:53.630 --> 00:40:59.440
in the warehouse like BigQuery.

00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:05.590
Once you're streaming your data
and also, data is ingested,

00:41:05.590 --> 00:41:10.160
pre-processed, and you store
your data into a data analytics

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:11.960
service like BigQuery.

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:13.940
You also have
older data you want

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:17.570
to aggregate or consolidate
into that warehouse.

00:41:17.570 --> 00:41:21.830
For example, you may have your
CRM, your customer profile,

00:41:21.830 --> 00:41:26.060
your purchase history, and
your inventory information.

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:29.750
All this data you want
to consolidate them

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:32.810
into the BigQuery data
warehouse service,

00:41:32.810 --> 00:41:36.820
and afterwards, you can consider
to leverage Google Cloud AI

00:41:36.820 --> 00:41:37.700
service.

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:40.090
For example, you may
use the Cloud Machine

00:41:40.090 --> 00:41:43.790
in the engine to build up
your own machine and model

00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:45.500
to forecast the demand.

00:41:45.500 --> 00:41:50.090
Or also, build a recommendation
engine on the TensorFlow model

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:55.670
to recommend some
popular items or to build

00:41:55.670 --> 00:41:58.510
a personalized recommendation
engine for your users.

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:04.570
So this is the
final architecture

00:42:04.570 --> 00:42:08.050
to incorporate the data
injection and the process

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:09.280
pipeline.

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:11.620
Cloud Pub/Sub ingests
the streaming data

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:14.770
from different sources
like the web server

00:42:14.770 --> 00:42:19.090
or from your accessed log.

00:42:19.090 --> 00:42:21.070
In both, streaming
data and the batch

00:42:21.070 --> 00:42:25.120
data can be pre-processed by
the data flow and aggregate

00:42:25.120 --> 00:42:28.930
into the data warehouse
service BigQuery

00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:32.860
And your data scientists
and business analysts

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:38.480
can use a holistic view of your
data to extract your customer's

00:42:38.480 --> 00:42:40.360
insight from BigQuery.

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:44.260
So Helen, would you like to
conclude our journey from one

00:42:44.260 --> 00:42:46.755
user to 100 million users?

00:42:46.755 --> 00:42:51.470
HELEN LO: This is a very
exciting architecture.

00:42:51.470 --> 00:42:53.570
Starting with our
single VM all the way up

00:42:53.570 --> 00:42:57.060
to this social architecture.

00:42:57.060 --> 00:42:59.420
So looking back
on our six stages,

00:42:59.420 --> 00:43:02.990
we started with a small
startup, just alpha phase,

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:06.170
all the way up to
100 million users.

00:43:06.170 --> 00:43:10.100
At stage two, we
decoupled our application.

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:13.280
We made our application
scalable and resilient.

00:43:13.280 --> 00:43:17.750
And at stage three, we leveraged
manage database services

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:21.650
like Cloud SQL and
Memorystore to have

00:43:21.650 --> 00:43:24.290
a better and stable data layer.

00:43:24.290 --> 00:43:28.280
And at stage 4, we wanted
to move our regional service

00:43:28.280 --> 00:43:29.780
into a global service.

00:43:29.780 --> 00:43:32.450
Global load
balancers, CDNs, cloud

00:43:32.450 --> 00:43:36.860
Spanners helped me to deploy
my application globally.

00:43:36.860 --> 00:43:40.310
And at stage five, we changed
the way our developers work,

00:43:40.310 --> 00:43:41.660
our engineers work.

00:43:41.660 --> 00:43:43.670
We moved from a
monolithic architecture

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:45.920
into a microservices
architecture,

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:51.750
and we added the CI/CD
practice into our architecture.

00:43:51.750 --> 00:43:56.180
So finally, stage 6, where we
started to collect our user

00:43:56.180 --> 00:44:00.110
data with the end goal of
understanding our user behavior

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:01.970
and our business insights.

00:44:01.970 --> 00:44:04.520
But I don't want
to think of stage 6

00:44:04.520 --> 00:44:06.920
as the end of a cloud journey.

00:44:06.920 --> 00:44:10.430
Instead, it is actually the
beginning of a data journey,

00:44:10.430 --> 00:44:12.680
and again I'm lost.

00:44:12.680 --> 00:44:15.020
I'm not sure where to
start on my data journey.

00:44:15.020 --> 00:44:17.410
So Harry, please help me.

00:44:17.410 --> 00:44:19.000
HARRY LIN: OK.

00:44:19.000 --> 00:44:22.450
So it's exciting to discuss
the next journey, the data

00:44:22.450 --> 00:44:24.790
journey on Google Cloud.

00:44:24.790 --> 00:44:29.570
But unfortunately, we will need
another session to discuss it.

00:44:29.570 --> 00:44:31.510
So don't worry, this
is the last slide

00:44:31.510 --> 00:44:33.160
of this break out session.

